### 2.2 Construction choice
**DOM/SVG tiles + frames (chosen):** one SVG scene; per tile a frame rect (enslaved colour, drawn behind, grown by frame thickness) + a tile rect + centred wrapped text label; two camp header groups; centre label groups with SVG paths for the arrows (dash draw-on for the free arrows, fade for the enslaved arrow) — all geometry from `stateAt(cfg,t)`, a pure function evaluable in node.
Rejected: Canvas 2D — loses per-element ink measurement + DOM label measurement the A6 fit leg needs. CSS keyframes — phase state not evaluable as a pure function (A11 receipts impossible). HTML div tiles — workable, but SVG gives exact rect/frame geometry for harness receipts at less code.
**Named deferrals (lawful, priced):** (1) the colonial-map lead-in (source 271–277 s) — a different visual device (antique map + highlight sweep), not the tile technique; the build opens on the paper ground at the tile lifecycle. (2) State-silhouette glyphs inside tiles — omitted: drawing state outlines from the specimen frames would approach tracing, and the technique's mechanic is area/frame re-categorisation, carried by tile size, frame, and the state NAME label. (3) The three-fifths banner/person at the window tail (source ≈298–305 s) — T219's technique, a separate queue item (#101).

